================================================================================
   COMPLETE GUIDE: CONNECTING ONE DESKTOP TO 2 WI-FI NETWORKS FOR ROS 2
================================================================================

================================================================================
PART 1 — UNDERSTANDING THE SETUP
================================================================================

Before touching anything, understand what we're building:

    Your Desktop PC
    ├── Wi-Fi Adapter #1 (built-in)   ──────► Network A (e.g., Tello drone)
    └── Wi-Fi Adapter #2 (USB dongle) ──────► Network B (e.g., second robot)

You need TWO physical Wi-Fi adapters:
  - One built-in + one USB dongle, OR
  - Two USB dongles

One adapter cannot reliably connect to two networks simultaneously.


================================================================================
PART 2 — HARDWARE REQUIREMENTS
================================================================================

What you need:
  [OK] Your desktop PC (with one built-in Wi-Fi or a PCIe card)
  [OK] 1x USB Wi-Fi adapter (dongle) — e.g., TP-Link TL-WN823N or any dual-band
  [OK] Two separate Wi-Fi networks/routers (or two devices broadcasting hotspots)

NOTE: If your desktop has NO built-in Wi-Fi, you need 2x USB Wi-Fi dongles.


================================================================================
PART 3 — LINUX SETUP (Ubuntu 22.04 / ROS 2 Humble assumed)
================================================================================

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1 — Plug in your USB Wi-Fi dongle
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Check that the system sees both adapters:

    ip link show

YOUR INTERFACES (already confirmed):
  - drone_a   (built-in, also known as wlp0s20f3 / wls5f3)
  - drone_b   (USB dongle, MAC: 28:87:ba:78:6c:3c)

Confirm with:

    iwconfig

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 2 — Install NetworkManager tools
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install network-manager net-tools wireless-tools

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 3 — Connect each adapter to its own network
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Connect drone_a to its network (Device A):

    nmcli device wifi connect "SSID_DRONE_A" password "password_A" ifname drone_a

Connect drone_b to its network (Device B):

    nmcli device wifi connect "SSID_DRONE_B" password "password_B" ifname drone_b

Replace SSID_DRONE_A, SSID_DRONE_B, password_A, password_B with your actual values.

Verify both are connected:

    nmcli device status

Both interfaces should show status: connected

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 4 — Check assigned IP addresses
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    ip addr show drone_a
    ip addr show drone_b

YOUR IPs (already confirmed):
  drone_a:  192.168.10.2    (network: 192.168.10.0/24,  gateway: 192.168.10.1)
  drone_b:  192.168.8.163   (network: 192.168.8.0/24,   gateway: 192.168.8.1)


================================================================================
PART 4 — ROUTING
================================================================================

NOTE: In your specific case, separate routing tables are NOT needed.
Your two networks are on different subnets (192.168.10.x vs 192.168.8.x),
so Linux already knows which interface to use for each device.

YOUR CONFIRMED ROUTING TABLE (from ip route show):

    default via 192.168.8.1  dev drone_b  metric 601     ← primary default (drone_b)
    default via 192.168.10.1 dev drone_a  metric 20602   ← fallback default (drone_a)
    192.168.8.0/24  dev drone_b  src 192.168.8.163       ← drone_b traffic
    192.168.10.0/24 dev drone_a  src 192.168.10.2        ← drone_a traffic

This means:
  - Traffic to 192.168.10.x automatically goes out through drone_a  [OK]
  - Traffic to 192.168.8.x  automatically goes out through drone_b  [OK]

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Verification — ping each device's gateway to confirm routing works
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    ping -I drone_a 192.168.10.1    # ping drone_a gateway
    ping -I drone_b 192.168.8.1     # ping drone_b gateway

If both reply, routing is working correctly. Proceed to Part 5.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT — drone_a IP lease warning
------------------------------------------------------------------------

‼️Your drone_a IP (192.168.10.2) has a limited DHCP lease (~1 hour at setup time).
If it changes after reconnecting, your FastDDS XML config will break.

To fix: reserve the IP in your router/device by binding MAC c8:58:c0:39:4e:05
to 192.168.10.2 in its DHCP settings.


================================================================================
PART 5 — ROS 2 CONFIGURATION
================================================================================

YOUR NETWORK SUMMARY (confirmed):
  drone_a  →  IP: 192.168.10.2    gateway: 192.168.10.1   (Tello / Device A)
  drone_b  →  IP: 192.168.8.163   gateway: 192.168.8.1    (Device B)

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Understanding ROS 2 networking
------------------------------------------------------------------------

ROS 2 uses DDS (Data Distribution Service) for communication.
By default, it uses multicast UDP which may go out the wrong interface.
You need to bind each ROS 2 instance/node to the correct interface.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1 — Set ROS_DOMAIN_ID per network
------------------------------------------------------------------------
‼️more details
ROS 2 uses domain IDs to separate communication spaces.
Use DIFFERENT domain IDs for each network:

    # For nodes communicating on drone_a (192.168.10.x network)
    export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=10

    # For nodes communicating on drone_b (192.168.8.x network)
    export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=20

Nodes with the same ROS_DOMAIN_ID on the same network talk to each other.
Different IDs are fully isolated.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 2 — Create FastDDS XML config for drone_a
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    nano ~/fastdds_drone_a.xml

Paste this content (binds DDS to your drone_a IP: 192.168.10.2):

    <?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
    <profiles xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
      <transport_descriptors>
        <transport_descriptor>
          <transport_id>custom_udp_drone_a</transport_id>
          <type>UDPv4</type>
          <interfaceWhiteList>
            <address>192.168.10.2</address>
          </interfaceWhiteList>
        </transport_descriptor>
      </transport_descriptors>
      <participant profile_name="drone_a_participant" is_default_profile="true">
        <rtps>
          <userTransports>
            <transport_id>custom_udp_drone_a</transport_id>
          </userTransports>
          <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
        </rtps>
      </participant>
    </profiles>

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 3 — Create FastDDS XML config for drone_b
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    nano ~/fastdds_drone_b.xml

Paste this content (binds DDS to your drone_b IP: 192.168.8.163):

    <?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
    <profiles xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
      <transport_descriptors>
        <transport_descriptor>
          <transport_id>custom_udp_drone_b</transport_id>
          <type>UDPv4</type>
          <interfaceWhiteList>
            <address>192.168.8.163</address>
          </interfaceWhiteList>
        </transport_descriptor>
      </transport_descriptors>
      <participant profile_name="drone_b_participant" is_default_profile="true">
        <rtps>
          <userTransports>
            <transport_id>custom_udp_drone_b</transport_id>
          </userTransports>
          <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
        </rtps>
      </participant>
    </profiles>

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 4 — Launch nodes bound to specific interfaces
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Terminal 1 — Nodes for drone_a (192.168.10.x network):

    export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=10
    export FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=~/fastdds_drone_a.xml
    ros2 run your_package your_node_for_drone_a

Terminal 2 — Nodes for drone_b (192.168.8.x network):

    export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=20
    export FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=~/fastdds_drone_b.xml
    ros2 run your_package your_node_for_drone_b


================================================================================
PART 6 — SENDING DIFFERENT COMMANDS TO EACH DEVICE
================================================================================

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Recommended architecture
------------------------------------------------------------------------

                    +-------------------------+
                    |    Master Controller    |
                    |   (Your ROS 2 node)     |
                    +--------+--------+-------+
                             |        |
              +--------------+        +--------------+
              v                                       v
   /drone_a/cmd_vel                       /drone_b/cmd_vel
   (Domain ID 10)                         (Domain ID 20)
   (FastDDS -> drone_a)                   (FastDDS -> drone_b)
   (192.168.10.2)                         (192.168.8.163)
              |                                       |
              v                                       v
        [Device A]                             [Device B]
      192.168.10.x                           192.168.8.x

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example: a dual-device commander node (dual_commander.py)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    import rclpy
    from rclpy.node import Node
    from geometry_msgs.msg import Twist

    class DualCommander(Node):
        def __init__(self):
            super().__init__('dual_commander')

            # Publisher for drone_a (192.168.10.x network)
            # Run this node with: ROS_DOMAIN_ID=10 + fastdds_drone_a.xml
            self.pub_a = self.create_publisher(Twist, '/drone_a/cmd_vel', 10)

            self.timer = self.create_timer(0.1, self.send_commands)

        def send_commands(self):
            cmd_a = Twist()
            cmd_a.linear.x = 0.5   # Move forward

            self.pub_a.publish(cmd_a)
            self.get_logger().info('Sent command to drone_a (192.168.10.x)')

    def main():
        rclpy.init()
        node = DualCommander()
        rclpy.spin(node)

    if __name__ == '__main__':
        main()

Run a separate identical node (new terminal, Domain 20 + fastdds_drone_b.xml) for drone_b.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Alternative: use a domain bridge node
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Install the domain bridge:

    sudo apt install ros-humble-domain-bridge

Create bridge.yaml:

    topics:
      /drone_a/cmd_vel:
        type: geometry_msgs/msg/Twist
        from_domain: 0      # Your internal domain
        to_domain: 10       # drone_a domain (192.168.10.x)

      /drone_b/cmd_vel:
        type: geometry_msgs/msg/Twist
        from_domain: 0
        to_domain: 20       # drone_b domain (192.168.8.x)

Run the bridge:

    ros2 run domain_bridge domain_bridge bridge.yaml


================================================================================
PART 7 — TESTING & VERIFICATION CHECKLIST
================================================================================

1. Both interfaces connected?
       nmcli device status
       (look for drone_a and drone_b both showing "connected")

2. Both have IP addresses?
       ip addr show drone_a    # expect: 192.168.10.2
       ip addr show drone_b    # expect: 192.168.8.163

3. Can you ping each device's gateway?
       ping -I drone_a 192.168.10.1   # drone_a gateway
       ping -I drone_b 192.168.8.1    # drone_b gateway

4. Can you ping each actual device?
       ping -I drone_a 192.168.10.xxx   # replace xxx with your Device A IP
       ping -I drone_b 192.168.8.xxx    # replace xxx with your Device B IP

5. ROS domain isolation working?
       ROS_DOMAIN_ID=10 ros2 topic list   # should show drone_a topics only
       ROS_DOMAIN_ID=20 ros2 topic list   # should show drone_b topics only

6. Topics visible on correct domain?
       ROS_DOMAIN_ID=10 ros2 topic echo /drone_a/cmd_vel
       ROS_DOMAIN_ID=20 ros2 topic echo /drone_b/cmd_vel


================================================================================
PART 8 — COMMON PITFALLS
================================================================================

  PROBLEM                          | CAUSE                          | FIX
  ---------------------------------|--------------------------------|-----------------------------
  Both devices on same topic       | Same ROS_DOMAIN_ID             | Use different domain IDs
  Commands go to wrong device      | Wrong FastDDS XML config loaded | Check FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_...
  Can ping one device but not other| Routing table issue            | Redo Part 4 routing steps
  USB Wi-Fi drops connection       | Power management               | sudo iwconfig wlan1 power off
  Routes lost after reboot         | Not persistent                 | Add crontab entry (Part 4)
  ROS nodes can't find each other  | DDS multicast on wrong iface   | Whitelist IP in FastDDS XML


================================================================================
PART 9 — QUICK REFERENCE: SHELL ALIASES
================================================================================

Add these to ~/.bashrc for convenience:

    # Activate Network A environment for ROS 2
    alias ros-net-a='export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=10 && export FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=~/fastdds_net_a.xml'

    # Activate Network B environment for ROS 2
    alias ros-net-b='export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=20 && export FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=~/fastdds_net_b.xml'

    # Quick check of both Wi-Fi interfaces
    alias check-wifi='nmcli device status && ip addr show wlan0 && ip addr show wlan1'

Then reload your shell:

    source ~/.bashrc

Usage:
    ros-net-a   (then run your Device A nodes)
    ros-net-b   (then run your Device B nodes)
    
    
    



